From: Bryan B <bbigari@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 14:38:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Import Bing grounding queries as AI Visibility prompts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Grounding queries are the phrases an AI actually used when it retrieved and
cited a page — the only first-party record of that anywhere, and a far better
basis for choosing what to track than syntax inferred from Search Console.

Bing exposes them in its web app only: the published Webmaster API has no
method for them, and the app's own endpoint authenticates with a session
cookie and CSRF token, so this app cannot fetch them itself. The owner
exports from their own browser and pastes the result here.

Both routes people actually have are accepted — the CSV download and the raw
JSON response — because their field names differ ("Grounding query" versus
GroundingQuery, "Citation share" versus CitationRate). Columns match on a
normalized heading rather than exact text, and a rate Bing omits stays null
so it renders as "—" rather than 0%, which would read as never cited.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
